# Deployment checklist — Generic Hosting

## What changed to get here

- **Rate limiting** (`app/api/app.py`): every `POST /api/chat` request
  is limited two ways — by IP (the real defense; default `20/minute`,
  `300/hour`) and by session (a looser secondary throttle; default
  `15/minute`). Session-based limiting is NOT a security boundary on
  its own — session IDs are client-generated and trivially spoofable
  (a bad actor can mint a fresh UUID every request), which is exactly
  why the IP limit exists as the real backstop. Tested explicitly in
  `tests/test_rate_limiting.py::test_rotating_session_ids_still_hit_ip_limit`.
- **Proxy-aware client IPs** (`app/api/app.py`, `TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT`):
  the per-IP limit above is only worth having if it counts real
  visitors. Behind Passenger/nginx every request arrives from the
  proxy's own address, so without `ProxyFix` the "20 per minute" cap
  was a single site-wide bucket shared by every visitor — a handful of
  concurrent conversations would have started returning 429s to
  everyone. `TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT` must match the real number of proxy
  hops: too low hides the client IP, and any value above 0 with NO
  proxy in front lets a caller forge `X-Forwarded-For` and bypass the
  limit entirely. Both directions are covered in
  `tests/test_rate_limiting.py`.
- **Prompt-injection defense, both directions**:
  - Inbound (`app/rag/input_guard.py`): clear override/extraction
    attempts are refused at the API layer before any embedding or LLM
    call is made, so an attacker can't burn API budget probing.
  - Outbound (`app/rag/guardrails.py` rule 2): any 8+ word verbatim run
    shared with the system prompt is replaced before it reaches the
    visitor, regardless of how the model was talked into producing it.
  Neither layer is sufficient alone — the inbound one can be worded
  around, the outbound one only acts after generation.
- **Ungrounded proper-noun guardrail** (`app/rag/guardrails.py` rule 4):
  generalizes the existing number-grounding rule to invented client
  names, case studies, and team members — the other category the system
  prompt calls out as never-fabricate.
- **WSGI entry point** (`wsgi.py`): this file used to be a byte-for-byte
  copy of `app/api/app.py`, which defines `create_app()` and no module-
  level `application` object — meaning the app could not have booted
  under Passenger at all. It is now a real entry point that loads
  `.env`, configures logging, and exports both `application` and `app`.
- **DB connection pooling** (`app/db.py`): `get_connection()` now
  borrows from a `DBUtils` `PooledDB` pool instead of opening a raw
  `pymysql.connect()` every call. Every request, every background lead-
  capture thread, and the scheduler job all call this — without
  pooling, that's a fresh TCP+auth round-trip on each one, which risks
  hitting a shared-hosting connection cap outright under concurrent
  traffic instead of degrading gracefully.
- **Non-blocking lead extraction** (`app/api/app.py`): moved off the
  request path onto a bounded background executor
  (`LEAD_CAPTURE_WORKERS`, default 4), so a visitor's response time no
  longer includes a second sequential LLM call. Bounded rather than a
  thread-per-request: each task holds a pooled DB connection for the
  length of an extraction call, so unbounded background work could
  drain the pool and stall the request path it was moved off.
- **Cached KB decode** (`app/kb/retrieval.py`): scoring a query used to
  re-read every `kb_chunks` row (title + content + a 3KB embedding blob,
  ~195KB per request) and re-run `struct.unpack` plus norm and title
  tokenization over all of it. Chunks are now decoded once into a
  process-level cache with norms and title tokens precomputed, and
  freshness is checked per request with one cheap aggregate query. In a
  synthetic benchmark at this KB's size that's ~195KB -> ~1KB read per
  request and ~2.3x less CPU on the scoring path. Worth knowing: this
  is not what a visitor feels — end-to-end latency is dominated by the
  DashScope call, not by this — it's a DB-load and memory-churn fix.
  An ingest run changes the fingerprint, so the cache refreshes itself
  with no restart needed.
- **Cache-augmented generation** (`app/rag/qa_cache.py`, `qa_cache`
  table): a repeat of an already-answered, context-free question is
  served from cache. An exact-text hit costs zero API calls; a semantic
  hit costs the embedding call the turn was paying anyway and skips the
  LLM. FAQ-shaped traffic ("what do you offer", "do you work outside
  Egypt") is where the savings land.
  Read the module docstring before tuning it — what is deliberately NOT
  cached is the important half: follow-up questions, anything echoing
  visitor-supplied numbers or names, guardrail-sanitized answers, and
  questions containing contact details. Cached answers are scoped to the
  KB signature and model pair that produced them, so a re-ingest retires
  them all on the next turn with no cache-clearing step.
  `QA_CACHE_SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD` is the risk knob: too low and a
  visitor gets a confident answer to a question they didn't ask. It
  defaults conservatively (0.95, near-paraphrases only) and should be
  calibrated against real traffic before being loosened;
  `QA_CACHE_SEMANTIC_ENABLED=false` keeps exact-text matching only.
- **Debounced lead emails** (`app/leads/service.py` +
  `app/api/app.py`'s scheduler): the "ready lead" email now waits for
  3 minutes of conversation quiet before sending, instead of firing on
  whatever was filled in at the exact moment a lead crossed the ready
  bar.

## Single-process assumption — read this before choosing a hosting plan/config

Three pieces of this app assume **one worker process**:

1. **Rate limiting** — default storage is in-process memory
   (`RATE_LIMIT_STORAGE_URI=memory://`). Two workers means two
   independent counters, so the real-world limit doubles (etc. per
   worker) without you changing a number anywhere.
2. **The debounced-lead-email scheduler** — runs as a background thread
   inside the Flask process. Two workers means two schedulers, means
   duplicate emails to the sales team.
3. **The DB connection pool** — sized per-process. Two workers means
   2x `DB_POOL_MAX_CONNECTIONS` actual connections against your MySQL
   plan's real limit.

A standard Passenger deployment for an
app like this typically runs as a single process, so this should be
fine as-is. If you ever explicitly configure multiple worker processes
(e.g. a future move to a VPS with gunicorn `--workers > 1`), all three
of the above need real fixes first: Redis-backed rate-limit storage,
an externally-owned single scheduler (cron hitting a dedicated
endpoint, or `APScheduler`'s built-in distributed lock support), and a
correspondingly smaller `DB_POOL_MAX_CONNECTIONS` per worker.

## Before you deploy — checklist

- [ ] **Check your MySQL plan's `max_connections` limit**
  and set `DB_POOL_MAX_CONNECTIONS` in `.env` with real
  headroom under it — leave room for phpMyAdmin or anything else
  sharing that database.
- [ ] Run `migrate_qa_cache.py` against the **production** database to
  create the `qa_cache` table. Without it every turn logs a cache write
  failure and falls back to a normal (uncached) answer — the bot still
  works, it just saves nothing.
- [ ] Run `migrate_leads_columns.py` (or confirm `schema.sql` is fully
  applied) against the **production** database — the `notified_at`/
  `updated_at` columns must exist before lead capture will work at all.
- [ ] Set every env var in `.env.example` in your hosting environment
  variables screen (or upload a real `.env` alongside
  `wsgi.py` — confirm which one your setup actually reads;
  Passenger apps typically pick up a `.env` in the app root via
  `python-dotenv`, which `wsgi.py` already calls).
- [ ] Set `FRONTEND_ORIGIN` to the real site domain(s), not `*`.
- [ ] Set `TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT` to the real number of proxy hops (`1`
  for a standard Passenger/nginx setup, `0` if nothing proxies the
  app). Verify it after deploying: hit the API from two different
  networks and confirm the Passenger log shows two different client
  IPs, not the proxy's address twice.
- [ ] **Re-run KB ingestion** if `knowledge_base.md` changed
  since the last deploy — editing the markdown does nothing on its own.
  Until `python scripts/ingest_kb.py knowledge_base.md` runs
  against the production database, `kb_chunks` still serves the old
  text, including any section that was deliberately removed.
- [ ] Set `SMTP_USER`/`SMTP_PASSWORD`/`SMTP_FROM_EMAIL` to a real
  mailbox — without these, lead/escalation emails silently log a
  warning and never send (chat itself still works fine).
- [ ] In your hosting Python App screen, confirm the "Application entry
  point" / "Application startup file" fields point at `wsgi.py` /
  `application`. `wsgi.py` now genuinely exports both `app` and
  `application`, so either name works — verified with
  `python -c "import wsgi; print(wsgi.application)"` from the app root
  with the venv active. (It previously exported neither, so if a past
  deploy of this app never started, that was why.)
- [ ] `pip install -r requirements.txt` inside the Passenger-managed
  virtualenv (via hosting's "Run Pip Install" or SSH into the venv it
  creates) — new dependencies since the last deploy: `flask-cors`,
  `APScheduler`, `Flask-Limiter`, `DBUtils`.
- [ ] After first deploy, hit `GET /api/health` from a browser to confirm the app is actually up
  before wiring the frontend at it.
- [ ] Watch the Passenger log file (set in hosting's Python App screen)
  for the first few real conversations — `logging.basicConfig` in
  `wsgi.py` now ensures INFO-level logs (including the scheduler job
  running) are visible there, not just warnings/errors.

## What's still NOT handled, flagged rather than silently skipped

- **Multiple worker processes** — see above; not needed unless traffic
  actually requires it, but don't turn this on without the follow-up
  fixes.
- **True horizontal scaling / high traffic** — this stack's real
  ceiling is the DashScope LLM call latency and quota, not this app's
  own code. No amount of backend tuning here changes that; if traffic
  ever gets large enough to matter, that's a provider/quota
  conversation, not a rate-limiting one.
- **Semantic (non-verbatim) prompt leaks** — the outbound exfiltration
  rule is a verbatim-run detector. A model that genuinely paraphrases
  its instructions in fresh wording slips past it. Catching that would
  need a second LLM call per turn, which isn't worth the latency and
  cost for the residual risk. Documented, with a test that pins the
  boundary (`test_pipeline_catches_indirect_system_prompt_leak_via_summary_framing`).
- **Corrections to already-filled lead fields** — the update-notification
  path only fires on blank -> filled transitions, so a visitor changing
  a budget they already gave doesn't re-notify the team. Pre-existing,
  unchanged here.